# ~/.config/kanshi/config
#
# Display profiles, applied at login and re-applied on hotplug. river 0.3 does
# not manage outputs itself — this is the only thing that does.
#
# WHY SCALE 1.25
#
# eDP-1 is 2560x1600 across a 340mm-wide panel — about 191 DPI. Unscaled, that
# renders everything at roughly half the size you are used to. Plasma was
# already compensating: its GTK config carries `gtk-xft-dpi=122880`, which is
# 120 DPI, i.e. exactly 1.25x. Matching that means river and Plasma look the
# same rather than the session switch changing every measurement on screen.
#
# 1.25 gives an effective 2048x1280. If it still reads small, 1.5 is the next
# sane step; wlroots handles fractional scale fine. Try it live first:
#     wlr-randr --output eDP-1 --scale 1.5
#
# Mode is deliberately NOT pinned. The panel offers 2560x1600 at both 165Hz and
# 60Hz and kanshi will keep the preferred (165Hz). If battery life matters more
# than smoothness on a given trip, add `mode 2560x1600@60` to this profile —
# high refresh is a real drain on a laptop that leaves the house.

profile laptop {
    output eDP-1 enable scale 1.25
}

# --- external displays ------------------------------------------------------
# Placeholder for the projector case, which `01-machines/thinkpad-fedora-windows.md`
# lists as a real use of this machine (presentations).
#
# Do NOT guess the output name or resolution — plug the thing in, run
# `wlr-randr`, and write what it actually reports. A profile that doesn't match
# is silently skipped, which looks identical to kanshi not running.
#
